							<table width="100%" border="0"><tr>
								<td align="left" valign="middle"><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=89.msg2155#msg2155"><img src="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/Themes/safgrey105/images/post/xx.gif" alt="" border="0" /></a></td>
								<td align="left" valign="middle">
									<b><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=89.msg2155#msg2155">Re: &quot;Questioning Freedom&quot; mentions Geologic/Blue Scholars</a></b>
									<div class="smalltext">&#171; <b>Reply #45 on:</b> October 22, 2006, 09:10:45 pm &#187;</div></td>
								<td align="right" valign="bottom" height="20" nowrap="nowrap" style="font-size: smaller;">
								</td>
							</tr></table>
							<hr width="100%" size="1" class="hrcolor" />
							<div style="overflow: auto; width: 100%;">Word to deuce.&nbsp; I agree.&nbsp; Dawson I&#039;m glad you&#039;re willing to voice your ideas and opinions on here, and aren&#039;t too intimidated to be a lone voice.&nbsp; I&#039;m more on the other side of things from what you&#039;re arguing, but only somewhat.&nbsp; I would like to know if you&#039;ve studied the history of dissenting political movements in this country, especially of minority background, or the history of the U.S. government&#039;s treatment of unions and the labor movement.&nbsp; The track record for our government in how it treats these groups isn&#039;t just bad, it&#039;s abominable.&nbsp; The concern here, I think, is that, given this track record, the questioning and visits from the FBI aren&#039;t just questions and visits, they are practices that have often been precursors of more serious issues and behaviors by the government against these groups.&nbsp; If the group being visited was an Arab charity with historic links to Hamas, I wouldn&#039;t agree with your argument necessarily, but it would make a lot more sense.&nbsp; In this case Bayan USA has nothing, in its approach or goals, that have any logical reason to be considered suspect by the US government, except for, and Geo correct me if I&#039;m wrong, the removal of the current extremely corrupt regime ruling the Phillipines right now.&nbsp; The only reason that would be suspect to the US government is because it considers the Phillipine government an ally in the War on Terror, and for political reasons it wouldn&#039;t want domestic groups causing political problems there, even problems as simple as promoting a democratic removal of the current president.&nbsp; And you can&#039;t think this is absurd.&nbsp; Our country has a long history, from early on up to contemporary times, of interfering in other country&#039;s politics for our own political reasons, as well as investigating, questioning, and detaining people domestically for the same reason.&nbsp; This isn&#039;t &quot;conspiracy theory&quot; this is history.&nbsp; This is why Geo, and most everyone else here is extremely concerned.&nbsp; I don&#039;t know that I agree with everything Bayan USA stands for, but I do believe everyone has the right to their ideas and opinions, without the interference or intimidation of their government.</div>
						</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td valign="bottom" class="smalltext" width="85%">
