							<hr width="100%" size="1" class="hrcolor" />
							<div style="overflow: auto; width: 100%;"><div class="quoteheader">Quote</div><div class="quote">Sorry, it&#039;s too late at night/morning for me to form a legit contribution to this discussion, but I just had to note that your accusation of incorrect information is in fact incorrect itself.&nbsp; During/shortly after the settlement of what would become California, local government sanctioned bounties (with reward) were put out on Native American heads, literally.&nbsp; If you brought in a severed head, you got paid quite nicely.&nbsp; In fact I think there were people achieving their whole subsistence based on this venture during that time.&nbsp; And yes this was post-Constitution.<br /><br />BAM</div><br />thanks. of this i was unaware. ignorance is defeated, one thread at a time.<br /><br /><div class="quoteheader">Quote</div><div class="quote">Also, are you saying the car bombings in the middle east are not terrorism?&nbsp; Because under your comparison they aren&#039;t, seeing as its merely one group killing another directly, which I&#039;m sure they would argue benefits them directly as well.&nbsp; I however would argue that this of course obviously instills fear within the targetted group, just as the Native American slaughter attempted to coerce cooperative &quot;relocating&quot; of Native American tribes through fear of death in the name of white settlement.&nbsp; Maybe cooperative &quot;relocating&quot; of Palestinians through fear?&nbsp; Probably a mixture of both<br /><br />my definition of terrorism would apply more to the violent action taken against civilians or bystanders as oppose to military persons.&nbsp; Actions against a military, whether by a military or not = military action.&nbsp; Actions against civilians/Native Americans/Jewish guy across the casbah/US citizens again by a military entity or not = terrorism.&nbsp; I haven&#039;t thought this out too far but I&#039;m focusing more on the target party, and either way I don&#039;t think your definition holds up historically to what we have called terrorism.<br /></div><br />hmm i would say the difference here is that the group engineering the car bombings (i&#039;m assuming you mean hamas and their ilk) can&#039;t hope to expel israel in open conflict, and is thus hoping to accomplish most of its goals through the inspiration of fear. colonial incursions in native america, however, weren&#039;t met with so stiff a challenge. natives were slaughtered in great numbers, and much of the retreating was out of necessity.<br /><br />note, i don&#039;t think what was done when europeans conquered the americas was any better than the behavior of suicide bombers in palestine or elsewhere. settlers in the americas perpetrated greater atrocities than those we see today in the middle east. i was simply arguing semantics. murder is bad, no matter what fancy name you give it.</div>
						</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td valign="bottom" class="smalltext" width="85%">
